CAT = "rsracTools"; SEG = "TLS"
Q = [
("Which instrument measures the insulation resistance of compressor motor windings to ground?",
 "Megohmmeter", ["Clamp-on ammeter", "Micron gauge", "Sling psychrometer"],
 "A megohmmeter applies a high test voltage and reads resistance in megohms, showing breakdown of winding insulation before it fails. A clamp meter reads current only."),
("Why does a flare connection need a 45-degree flaring tool rather than a plumbing flare tool?",
 "Refrigeration fittings seal at 45 degrees", ["Plumbing flares are always larger", "It cuts the tube square at once", "It work-hardens the copper less"],
 "SAE refrigeration flare fittings seal on a 45-degree face, so the flare must match that angle. A mismatched angle seals on a line and leaks."),
("Why is a torque wrench used when tightening a flare nut on a mini-split line set?",
 "Wrong torque causes leaks", ["It removes the need for oil", "It measures system pressure", "It replaces leak testing"],
 "Flare nuts have a torque value for each size. Too little leaves a leak path; too much thins or cracks the flare."),
("Why should vacuum hoses be short and of large diameter?",
 "Less restriction, faster evacuation", ["They hold more refrigerant", "They stop oil leaving the pump", "They resist higher pressure"],
 "Vacuum flow slows sharply in long, narrow hoses. Short, large-bore hoses with valve cores removed let the pump pull a deep vacuum faster."),
("Why are valve cores removed with a core tool before evacuating a system?",
 "Cores restrict the vacuum flow", ["Cores contain the oil charge", "They are replaced at every visit", "They block the micron gauge"],
 "A Schrader core is a major restriction during evacuation. A core removal tool takes it out under pressure and reinstalls it afterwards without losing the vacuum."),
("Where should the micron gauge be connected to read the system's true vacuum?",
 "Far end, away from the pump", ["Directly on the pump inlet", "On the recovery cylinder", "On the charging scale's hose"],
 "A gauge at the pump reads the pump's pull, not the system's. Placing it on the system end, isolated from the pump during the decay test, shows what the system holds."),
("What does a digital refrigerant scale do during charging?",
 "Weighs the refrigerant charge added", ["Measures liquid line pressure", "Shows evaporator superheat", "Detects moisture in the oil"],
 "A charging scale tracks the weight of refrigerant leaving the cylinder, so the nameplate or calculated charge can be weighed in accurately."),
("Why must recovery equipment used on an A2L system be rated for A2L refrigerants?",
 "It must not ignite the refrigerant", ["It recovers faster at low temperature", "It separates the oil automatically", "It works without a cylinder"],
 "A2L refrigerants have low flammability. Equipment rated for them limits sparks from switches and motors so recovered vapour cannot be ignited."),
("A condensing unit must be lifted onto a roof with a crane. Who signals the crane operator?",
 "One trained signaller", ["Anyone near the load", "Person holding the tag line", "Building owner"],
 "Only one trained, designated signaller directs the operator so signals are not mixed up. Tag lines control the load's swing; they are not for signalling."),
("A sling's rated capacity is reduced most when it is used how?",
 "At a low angle to the load", ["In a vertical hitch", "With a shackle at the hook", "On a padded load corner"],
 "As the sling angle to the horizontal decreases, the tension in each leg rises well above the load's weight. Low angles can overload a sling rated for the load."),
("Which drawing shows where rooftop units sit in relation to the roof structure?",
 "Mechanical plan view", ["Electrical riser diagram", "Ladder wiring diagram", "Refrigerant piping isometric"],
 "A plan view shows equipment locations from above against the building grid. Wiring diagrams and isometrics show circuits and pipe routing, not placement on the roof."),
("A specification calls for a 'high efficiency' unit, but the drawing lists a standard model. What should you do?",
 "Ask the designer to clarify", ["Install the cheaper model", "Install whichever arrives first", "Change the specification yourself"],
 "When documents conflict, the mechanic raises a request for information before installing, so the right equipment goes in and the record stays correct."),
("Why is copper refrigeration tubing kept capped until it is installed?",
 "Keeps out dirt and moisture", ["Stops it bending in transit", "Prevents it turning green", "Holds the factory nitrogen charge"],
 "Refrigerant systems need clean, dry tubing. Open ends collect dust and humid air, which become restrictions and moisture problems later."),
("How should a nitrogen cylinder be moved around a job site?",
 "Capped, upright on a cylinder cart", ["Rolled along on its side", "Dragged by its valve", "Carried by the regulator"],
 "High-pressure cylinders are moved with the protective cap on, upright on a cart and secured. A broken valve can turn a cylinder into a projectile."),
("Why are floor and finishes protected before a furnace and coil are replaced in an occupied home?",
 "Avoid damage and dust", ["Building code requires a tarp", "It speeds up the brazing", "It keeps refrigerant off the coil"],
 "Work site preparation includes protecting the client's property from scratches, dust and debris. It is part of planning the job, not a code item."),
("What does a data logger left on a walk-in cooler record?",
 "Temperatures over time", ["Refrigerant charge weight", "Motor insulation resistance", "Oil acid level"],
 "A logger records temperature, and sometimes humidity or pressure, at set intervals. The trend shows cycling, door openings and defrost problems a single reading misses."),
]
